WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.335
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.330
NARRATOR: The typical candidate
for a knee replacement

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.300
has osteoarthritis, where
the cartilage in the knee

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.860
has worn away.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.840
Knee replacement
surgery restores

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.850
the worn areas with an implant.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.070
To help place the
implant properly,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:20.780
the surgeon can
use an instrument

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.600
custom designed for the
patient's damaged anatomy.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.060
A successful knee replacement
depends on its alignment

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.360
with the femur and tibia,
the upper and lower leg bones

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.130
that connect at the knee.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.650
That's a challenge
because knee implants

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.490
are standard, while every
patient's bones are unique.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.520
So starting with an MRI
scan of the patient's knee,

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.590
this medical device
company produces

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.670
custom-made surgical templates,
plastic devices the surgeon

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.000
uses to sculpt the surface
of the patient's bones

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.640
to align the knee
implant accurately.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.760
The surgeon no longer has to
visually calculate the bone

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.850
cuts on the spot in the
operating room, which takes

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.370
more time and is more invasive.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.280
An engineer uses the
MRI scan to create

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.880
3D models of the
patient's femur and tibia.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.330
The patient's cartilage has
worn out in between these bones,

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:17.480
causing painful rubbing that
erodes the bone surface.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.200
The implant effectively replaces
the damaged bone and cartilage.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.210
Once each bone model is
ready, another engineer

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.690
applies a 3D model of
the correct size implant,

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.840
then, following directions
the surgeon has provided,

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.870
performs virtual surgery
on the bone model.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.510
This will properly align
the implant to the patient's

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.570
specific anatomy and fit
to the angle at which

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:42.995
that particular knee moves.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.820
During this surgery
simulation, the computer

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.250
records every bone cut.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.150
The surgeon reviews and approves
the surgical plan only once

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.670
before manufacturing begins.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.400
The computer which
recorded the bone cuts

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.640
guides a 3D printer to
produce plastic templates

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.650
for both the femur and tibia.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.920
The surgeon will use these
to repeat the exact same cuts

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.180
in real life.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.690
The 3D printer applies a
layer of powdered plastic,

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.700
then, following
the computer model,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.890
solidifies select
areas with the laser.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.910
This process keeps
repeating building

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.110
up the shape of these surgical
templates layer by layer.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.080
One printer run produces
personalized templates for

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.700
up to 50 patients at a time.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.210
So the laser inscribes
the patient's name

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.530
and identification
code on each part.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.280
Once the printing
is done, it's just

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.085
a matter of breaking up the
unsolidified powdered plastic.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.250
And fishing out the templates.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.570
They brush off the bulk
of the remaining powder.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.130
The posts on the
bottom are the points

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.325
at which pins affix the template
to the bone during surgery.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.940
Next, the templates go
into a tumbler containing

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.545
plastic balls and air jets.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.090
As the drum rotates for 20
minutes, the balls knock off,

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:37.895
and the air jets blow off
most of the remaining powder.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.900
The templates exit
the tumbler 90% clean.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.000
They go through two
wash cycles to remove

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.670
the last remnants of powder.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.040
Before packaging,
they'll be sterilized.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.820
But first, every
template must pass

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.730
a quality control inspection.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.100
Using a laser, a
technician scans

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.960
it to make sure
the manufacturing

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.280
process produced an exact
match to the patient's bone.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.780
This model shows how the femur
and tibia form the knee joint

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.720
and how the surgeon secures
the customized template

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.030
onto each of these bones.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.280
Thanks to these
devices, the surgeon

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.650
doesn't have to figure out
the bone cuts on the fly

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.300
in the operating room.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.780
They're all pre-calculated
by the computer model.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.820
This method eliminates
several steps

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.230
required in standard surgery.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.850
The operation takes
less time and requires

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.120
half as many instruments
to set up and clean up.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.890
And because the
surgeon no longer

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.530
has to drill a hole in the bone
to check the knee implant's

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.870
alignment, the
patient experiences

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.140
less pain and bleeding
and a smaller chance

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:01.100
of developing complications.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

